\begin{abstract}
% D025: web contamination prevalence context
Web-scale text corpora are increasingly contaminated by recursive synthetic content---text recursively generated across multiple generations, with recent estimates suggesting that 10--40\% of content on some platforms is machine-generated~\cite{sun2025aigt, spennemann2025quantifying}. Information degrades monotonically per the Data Processing Inequality with each generation, yet no method estimates a document's generational depth. We introduce the first systematic framework for generational depth estimation. Our approach extracts 15 statistical features and classifies depth via Ordinal Binary Decomposition (OBD) with LightGBM, preserving ordinal structure. We empirically measure the discrimination boundary $\Kempirical$, the maximum depth at which adjacent generations remain statistically distinguishable. Across 4 model-mode configurations and 4 decoding strategies (8 conditions total) at the $\leq$1.5B parameter scale, we find $\Kempirical=2$ is consistent: bootstrap 95\% CI lower bounds for 1-vs-2 across the four model-mode configurations all exceed $\theta=0.60$ while all 2-vs-3 CI upper bounds fall below it; decoding strategies likewise yield $\auc$ above $\theta$ at 1-vs-2 and below at 2-vs-3. Cross-model transfer shows classifiers retain 80--92\% accuracy across continuation-mode generators, with binary (0-vs-1) $\auc$ exceeding 0.96, indicating depth features are largely text-intrinsic. Preliminary neural probing on one dataset yields $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}=3$, suggesting our 15-feature $\Kempirical=2$ is a representation-dependent lower bound. Frontier LLMs achieve ${\sim}88\%$ binary detection but only ${\sim}33\%$ five-class depth accuracy---below our OBD classifier's 38.6\%---confirming depth estimation as a non-trivial task requiring specialized features. % D025: C3 adjusted from independent claim to supporting validation
Our findings motivate a three-tier taxonomy (human / generation-1 / generation-2+) for depth-aware data curation; $\Kempirical=2$ correctly predicts that graduated refinement beyond the binary boundary provides no additional benefit---binary filtering at the $\Kempirical$ boundary ($0.5941$ MMLU) outperforms both graduated depth weighting and surprisal-based selection ($0.5894$), establishing $\Kempirical$ as an actionable curation guideline.
\end{abstract}

% === Section 1: Introduction ===
\input{introduction}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Interpretation.}
The consistency of $\Kempirical = 2$ across all 8 tested configurations indicates that the two-step information bottleneck is a structural feature of autoregressive generation at the $\leq$1.5B scale.
The feature degradation analysis provides mechanistic evidence: surprisal-variance features degrade in a shared order across model families (Spearman $\rho \geq 0.85$), suggesting that each generation step smooths the token-level surprisal landscape in a model-independent fashion.
The neural probe result ($\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$ on one dataset) confirms that the 15D boundary is representation-dependent, not fundamental---$\Ktheory \geq 3$.
Extended theoretical discussion, including connections to model collapse theory and scale considerations, appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion}.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The implied three-tier taxonomy (human / generation-1 / generation-2+) requires no per-model or per-decoding-strategy calibration.
Cross-model transfer (80--92\% accuracy retention; Section~\ref{sec:transfer}) enables deployment without generator-specific retraining.
Our filtering experiments (Section~\ref{sec:filtering}) show that binary filtering at the $\Kempirical{=}2$ boundary is the most effective strategy among those tested; graduated strategies offer no additional benefit.
Frontier LLMs achieve ${\sim}88\%$ binary detection but only ${\sim}33\%$ five-class depth accuracy (below OBD's 38.6\%), confirming that depth estimation requires specialized features (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_llm}).

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our findings are bounded by (1)~model scale ($\leq$1.5B), (2)~prompt-chain rather than retrain-chain protocols (our $\Kempirical$ is a conservative upper bound on retrain-chain $\Kstar$ under monotonic-degradation assumptions), (3)~English C4 text only, (4)~four decoding strategies, (5)~threshold sensitivity ($\Kempirical$ shifts from 3 at $\theta{=}0.55$ to 1 at $\theta{=}0.70$; our conclusions are conditioned on $\theta{=}0.60$), (6)~marginal boundary (Qwen-continuation 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.15\,pp), (7)~the 15D feature set yields an operational lower bound ($\Ktheory$ may be strictly higher), and (8)~the neural probe result ($\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}{=}3$) covers a single dataset and awaits cross-model validation.
Detailed limitation analysis appears in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}.

\subsection{Extended Limitations}
\label{sec:extended_limitations}

\emph{Prompt-chain versus retrain-chain.}
Our prompt-chain protocol holds model quality constant; real-world retrain-chains introduce additional degradation through training dynamics.
Under the monotonic-degradation assumption~\cite{shumailov2024collapse}, $\Kstar^{\text{retrain}} \leq \Kstar^{\text{prompt}}$, making our $\Kempirical = 2$ a conservative estimate.

\emph{Threshold sensitivity.}
At $\theta = 0.55$, $\Kempirical = 3$ uniformly.
At $\theta = 0.65$, continuation-mode configurations drop to $\Kempirical = 1$ while rewrite retains $\Kempirical = 2$.
At $\theta = 0.70$, $\Kempirical = 1$ universally.
The boundary is robust within a practical range but not invariant.

\emph{Boundary marginality.}
Qwen-continuation's 1v2 CI lower bound (0.6015) exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.15\,pp; Qwen-rewrite's 2v3 CI upper bound (0.5991) falls below $\theta$ by 0.09\,pp.
All eight boundary comparisons yield permutation $p < 0.01$.

\emph{Graduated filtering.}
Graduated filtering at all tested $\alpha$ values (0.1--0.9) underperforms binary-ours on MMLU (best: $0.5892$ at $\alpha{=}0.9$ vs.\ $0.5941$), with a monotonic trend.
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, HellaSwag matches binary-ours exactly ($0.6701$), consistent with $\Kempirical = 2$ predicting no benefit from finer ordinal structure.

